  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The equation \n\n$$\nk({x}^{2}+7x+10)=5{x}^{2}+35x+50\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means that after distributing $k$ on the left-hand side, the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nk({x}^{2}+7x+10)=k{x}^{2}+7kx+10k\n$$\n\nThis must equal the right-hand side. So, we have\n\n$$\nk{x}^{2}+7kx+10k=5{x}^{2}+35x+50.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms. \n\nComparing the ${x}^{2}$ terms, we have\n\n$$\nk=5.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k=5$ produces the correct linear coefficient and constant term:\n\n- $7k=7(5)=35\\,\u2713$\n\n- $10k=10(5)=50\\,\u2713$\n\nSo, our solution is valid.",
